\begin{align*}
\mathbf{v} &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{y} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
We want to find the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto $\mathbf{y}$, which is given by
$$ \text{proj}_{\mathbf{y}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{y}}{\|\mathbf{y}\|^2} \mathbf{y} $$
First, we calculate the dot product $\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{y}$:
$$ \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{y} = (3)(0) + (-4)(1) + (0)(0) = 0 - 4 + 0 = -4 $$
Next, we calculate the squared norm of $\mathbf{y}$:
$$ \|\mathbf{y}\|^2 = (0)^2 + (1)^2 + (0)^2 = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1 $$
Then, we plug these values into the formula:
$$ \text{proj}_{\mathbf{y}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{-4}{1} \mathbf{y} = -4 \mathbf{y} = -4 \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -4 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} $$

\boxed{-4 \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}}
